{"title": "Introduction to Programming Language Foundations", "learningObjectives": [
    "Define the core components of a programming language specification, including syntax, semantics, and execution model.",
    "Explain how lexical analysis, scoping, and data model behavior govern program execution and state management.",
    "Apply structural verification and debugging methodologies to identify and resolve syntactic, runtime, and logical errors in code."
  ], "beats": [
    {
      "title": "Module Structure and IR Representation",
      "claim": "A programming language specification organizes code into modules containing global values, functions, and metadata that define the program's overall structure.",
      "example": "An LLVM module groups all global variables, function definitions, and type declarations into a single unit before compilation begins.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a hierarchical diagram showing a module node branching into global values, function nodes, and metadata nodes, with clear labels for each component.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Basic Blocks and Control Flow",
      "claim": "Functions are divided into basic blocks that execute sequentially until a terminator instruction redirects execution to a specific predecessor control-flow edge.",
      "example": "A conditional branch in a function splits into two basic blocks, each terminating with a jump instruction that routes to the next logical step.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a flowchart of a function with three basic blocks, highlighting the terminator instruction at the end of each block and arrows indicating control flow paths.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Phi Instructions and Value Selection",
      "claim": "Phi instructions select values according to the predecessor control-flow edge, enabling correct variable state tracking across merged execution paths.",
      "example": "When two branches converge, a phi instruction chooses the value from the branch that was actually executed during runtime.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a diamond-shaped control flow graph with a phi node at the merge point, labeled with the values from each incoming edge.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Explicit Memory Operations",
      "claim": "Load and store instructions make memory access explicit, requiring the compiler to track where data is read from and written to in the program's address space.",
      "example": "A store instruction assigns a computed value to a specific memory location, while a load instruction retrieves that value for further computation.",
      "visualDirection": "Illustrate a memory model with a register, a memory address, and arrows showing a store writing to memory and a load reading from it.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Structural Verification Rules",
      "claim": "Valid IR must satisfy structural and typing rules, and the verifier reports IR that violates those rules to catch errors before optimization begins.",
      "example": "The verifier checks that every basic block ends with a terminator and that all variables are properly typed before the compiler proceeds.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a checklist graphic with three boxes: structural rules, typing rules, and verifier output, showing a red 'X' on a rule violation.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compiler Work Separation",
      "claim": "Compiler work separates parsing and semantic analysis from lowering, verification, optimization, and target-specific code generation, allowing each phase to focus on distinct tasks",
      "example": "The parser converts source code into an abstract syntax tree, while the semantic analyzer checks types and scopes before the lowering phase begins.",
      "visualDirection": "Draw a linear pipeline diagram with five stages labeled parsing, semantic analysis, lowering, verification, optimization, and code generation, with arrows flowing left to right.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Lexical Analysis and Scoping",
      "claim": "Names are bound to objects within namespaces and scopes, and lexical analysis breaks source code into tokens that define the program's lexical structure.",
      "example": "A variable declared inside a function belongs to the function's local scope, while a global variable is bound to the module's namespace.",
      "visualDirection": "Show nested boxes representing global and local scopes, with variable names placed inside the appropriate scope box and arrows indicating binding relationships.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Data Model and Execution Model",
      "claim": "Python objects have identity, a type, and a value, and the execution model processes expressions to produce values while statements control execution and bindings.",
      "example": "An integer object has a unique memory address, a type tag, and a numeric value, while an assignment statement binds a name to that object.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a simple object diagram showing an object with three fields: identity (address), type, and value, alongside an assignment arrow binding a name to it.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Exception Handling and Control Flow",
      "claim": "Exceptions interrupt normal control flow until a matching handler processes them or they propagate, allowing programs to handle errors without halting execution.",
      "example": "When a division by zero occurs, an exception jumps out of the current block and is caught by a try-except handler that logs the error and continues.",
      "visualDirection": "Illustrate a control flow path with a normal execution line, a red 'exception' arrow jumping to a handler block, and a green 'propagate' arrow moving upward.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Debugging and Decomposition",
      "claim": "Debugging separates syntax, runtime, and logical failures, while decomposition and abstraction reduce the number of interacting concerns to simplify program analysis.",
      "example": "A syntax error is caught by the parser, a runtime error occurs during execution, and a logical error is fixed by breaking the code into smaller, testable functions.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a three-column table labeled syntax, runtime, and logical failures, with each column containing a brief example and a corresponding debugging tool icon.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    }
  ]}
